placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{rank}(A).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1869 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125404} :activity/id #uuid "92e4f0f3-75fd-5dde-9078-a3a161f5bd6a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125404 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125784 :tutorial/content "A linear transformation ![](images/93/9389ee0220ab92f8f743e705c7f9bef43f0f5c1e6122790e619ae64d99e4a480.svg) is **invertible** if there is another linear transformation ![](images/25/256c8d685bae4ddedcbfb4f972f5dcbaa65a00bf9853128bcf61a5f0fbc44052.svg) such that \n\n![](images/e4/e43ee86a504bad240bf68cc1110136b16fbb9ddb7bb250e4146582d928685821.svg)\n\nfor every vector ![](images/80/807f62e69b36dbb97ce90919d746dc86cd890eaeab263a25d6287af18af8a598.svg) The transformation ![](images/25/256c8d685bae4ddedcbfb4f972f5dcbaa65a00bf9853128bcf61a5f0fbc44052.svg) is called the **inverse** of ![](images/43/43a304a55ccd9c3f567bc9ca58aa2fb6b9aa0f8847f74a7c3dbbab3519729bb3.svg) An invertible linear transformation is also called a **bijection**. \n\nWe have the following theorem regarding invertible linear transformations: \n\n*If ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is the standard matrix of ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg), then ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg) is invertible if and only if ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is invertible*.\n\nRecall that the invertible matrix theorem in terms of dimension, rank, and nullity states that if ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is an ![](images/3e/3e214695b86d4170ea46f8cd0f8a4fd78e3d1c3a6abab1e8de1e3c35634c10a1.svg) matrix, then \n\n![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is invertible ![](images/14/14f4479e361c0db347e9c3da0135114e8b59f7a26d3533f48a89a248501d7d00.svg)\n![](images/28/281f907860a395891d590680f527a1eee8be046879da8f3607669a22cae5e5d2.svg)\n\nIn the theorem above, we can make the following swaps, where ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg) is a linear transformation with the standard matrix ![](images/70/70836ef8c789fb64a4b4a4b29a48074c146b8374116c1eeca4e3251def9e7d80.svg)\n![](images/96/9607abc75a2c67c4a11635cb9c84d6f575582d9961a822bdbf3d5e056536a9ce.svg)\n\nAs a result, we obtain the **invertible matrix theorem in terms of linear transformations:**\n\n ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg) is a bijection (invertible) ![](images/dc/dccddb0dd79fd52c99a28752fdcad781611226a29dc7f807411c349815f8442b.svg) \n![](images/14/14f4479e361c0db347e9c3da0135114e8b59f7a26d3533f48a89a248501d7d00.svg)  ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) is invertible" :tutorial/id #uuid "0d48d63e-a4e4-58a3-9520-cc4eaff29749" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3531 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6244a8ce-91c2-5c7a-8b6b-8f1b8c3887ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125405 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13"} :step/id #uuid "260959de-4a00-5de6-b086-941592cccc26" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f"} :step/id #uuid "cd09bb01-66a3-55db-bfd3-d51ed79661a9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352"} :step/id #uuid "0cb9a218-ad79-5dd3-b698-91a9f29f4e9d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125405}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124747} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417707} :activity/id #uuid "a321f5ff-d1ce-5778-90d0-3e0d34ad246d" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417705 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417704 :question/id #uuid "c77aeb41-578d-533f-87d1-e33216f35c1e"} :step/id #uuid "291db6d3-4728-529e-a9be-f15ca20db25d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125164 :question/id #uuid "a059dc0b-5296-5e25-93e6-3ffd38125f6c"} :step/id #uuid "3efa04eb-3c5c-549f-ac5a-28d6ca38cb8b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417705}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417703 :question/id #uuid "0464e7a7-ce22-55cd-90ec-4b6a71a12079"} :step/id #uuid "8823976c-e582-552f-a786-db586ba8c928" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417706}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417684} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491234} :activity/id #uuid "b46dcd91-903b-5d2e-987d-47f39b7bd951" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491234 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364213 :question/id #uuid "3c1b6231-ddc6-5ac3-a5c8-9d11b32158e5"} :step/id #uuid "492653ce-e0e3-57fb-97bc-5d7f36d893d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491240 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272288 :question/id #uuid "2fc1f275-1b92-5d39-91a9-c77fc478abd0"} :step/id #uuid "604d3714-832d-51bd-9fb5-c20fe1903624" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491262}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491133 :question/id #uuid "360fdc6a-8e68-5e59-be91-60b011ed6613"} :step/id #uuid "8188ea3f-faad-5417-8dd7-ce49d3aa7e10" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491240}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491262 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491115 :question/id #uuid "0cc9c978-a013-5a19-912d-5fcfcd8af303"} :step/id #uuid "b9c78f66-b08f-5798-8277-4bd48e8f9bee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272302 :question/id #uuid "2805dfb0-8d9a-5775-8051-a005b74b03a8"} :step/id #uuid "e7bbe3c7-afd2-571d-8c8c-ff79627d78d1"}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490508}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124330 :question/id #uuid "d135b6c2-8257-5ba5-ac15-3d43a9abd3d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5444" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is invertible? \n\nI. $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n\nII. $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=0$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/3e/3eba90e8e26e611656e00139533ea0e91628b7d53316fe0fbb33961d75211d04.svg) \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fe47a12-7b18-565d-9ace-87a94658a0d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121356} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6b5e35-e6e9-5cbc-ac74-9f56d9811e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121357} {:answer/id #uuid "2e47bab4-a18b-558e-86e4-0c9a1ada5bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121358} {:answer/id #uuid "69d3ca9d-4527-5bd4-b5b6-cd81f62ee538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121359} {:answer/id #uuid "72d715f0-2717-5cb2-a7cb-633f5d474954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121360}] :answer-field/id #uuid "879c3a0b-2230-5e76-b4a3-2f1cf5aeed34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121355}] :question/id #uuid "c2639497-e9ef-5687-af7f-94da5d96ad71" :question/math-academy-id "q-141484" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T: R^{5} → R^{5}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$, and $T(v_{1}) = T(v_{2})$ is true if and only if $v_{1} = v_{2}$?\n\n1. $Ker(T) = {0}$\n2. $T$ is invertible\n3. $Im(T) = R^{5}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "341579a5-829a-5c6f-b9ae-ac1104715fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477277} {:answer/id #uuid "f9528de3-82ee-51af-928d-d06047b50d5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477277} :answer-field/id #uuid "f21f4f59-ebb2-54ce-9b82-e47328aa57cf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477608} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa444f00-2edc-56ca-ac88-606941abc70e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477279} {:answer/id #uuid "246c1374-a564-5a2e-bac9-8087e79a76f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477279} :answer-field/id #uuid "c16758a4-1886-58ac-8f87-e81aa550b3ed" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477609} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ac9179d-a2ff-53d7-bec9-876c5f132474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477281} {:answer/id #uuid "617679dc-1aa7-52e3-9767-a5c125f6f463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477282} :answer-field/id #uuid "2870ac10-bdff-5d8b-af67-13e6e02c0504" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477610}] :question/id #uuid "d2b1c7be-1664-54ba-8015-a807eb2f1187" :question/math-academy-id "q-38033" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{8}\\to {R}^{8}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ for some distinct vectors  ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}?$ \n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$\n\nII. $T$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(T)<8$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{8}$ into ${R}^{8},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{8} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=8 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 8.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{rank}(T)<8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4f795a3-cd3e-5c24-bdd1-ff02322fb141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449544} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5f8ab7-4cce-5ec8-9a38-14eac636465a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :a