 that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is a linear transformation and $T({u}_{1})-T({u}_{2})=0,$ we have $T({u}_{1}-{u}_{2})=0.$ So ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3,$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "422924b4-52fe-531f-a431-2930ed3fcc1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458750} {:answer/id #uuid "46707c39-a729-5f37-b02d-6b6349de020e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458756} {:answer/id #uuid "80cb7339-73c7-5e3e-8a6c-b8959ca9790c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458854} {:answer/id #uuid "b663eb34-b00f-59e8-bb33-8fbde678b6de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458956} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fb6fad-edf8-5bd5-8196-28ca6189208d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458750} :answer-field/id #uuid "374d59dc-859f-58b1-91a1-44f364cb378e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459112}] :question/id #uuid "4841bd4c-e53a-5f1a-ae44-1f39564910ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-141485" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $T$ is not a bijection, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(T)=3$\n\nIII. $T(v)=0$ for some $v\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$ In turn, there is $v\\ne 0$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4431191c-327a-560c-ae2e-0a936d7eb7b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490640} {:answer/id #uuid "986f71ff-e7ec-55cd-9394-82b48c3bf3b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490825} {:answer/id #uuid "aed3b03d-3ae1-5ed6-9414-6be7b69ae7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490857} {:answer/id #uuid "ce62f560-86ad-52eb-8dca-7d8fcdf559c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490900} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2b2d92-434b-5242-adbe-1f59d584cccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490900} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4dca603-a431-59c9-afde-0218f7a30964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491095}] :question/id #uuid "0cc9c978-a013-5a19-912d-5fcfcd8af303" :question/math-academy-id "q-141487" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ if and only if ${v}_{1}={v}_{2},$  then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$\n\nII. $T$ is a bijection\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(T)=7$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ if and only if ${v}_{1}={v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ is a bijection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124632 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124436 :question/id #uuid "427d3dc9-9ae8-5c40-b75c-519db84f2bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9986" :question/problem "$\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection ![](images/29/29402a77d81267af571672df82b86e1d9853f17f26b6ad0a139a0d0f4ea906c7.svg) \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection ![](images/dd/dd3981fa517de2829a94c8b50c09e66a9f9830a3ddcf2651b583fb4da348c93c.svg) \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9b4e933-34c2-5650-bfd2-c7d46346c5ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "rank(T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121338} {:answer/id #uuid "f78c3d6b-8aa1-5e23-9a94-e06ba5bd733c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121339} {:answer/id #uuid "03c5a58f-9d94-5006-9f81-56ec64119e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121340} {:answer/id #uuid "0695bffc-24d7-5da1-8a85-90a545d5da95" :answer/type {:db/id