}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing expression are $\\{0\\}$ and $0.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1962 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158042} :activity/id #uuid "3a40bda5-f823-5841-986e-d65c61c1a55f" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158605 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to find the images of simple objects in the plane under the action of nonlinear transformations.\n\nConsider the square region ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) and its boundary ![](images/1a/1a8826befbd05114d1886fbe8fd3f83b2e0bd868bcdedd76f0fd1350451f3bde.svg) in the ![](images/13/13377a2a85887e0a15df06f28ec7e6be35b98579a0865d0292a7f64da0d57b2e.svg)-plane, where ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) is given by\n\n![](images/f2/f285f8016e64c5ab750d94dac618fe2c1a09ced6f5fce7bbaea85e7258eda2c5.svg)\n\nA sketch of this region, specified with a counterclockwise orientation, is shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n     \n\nSince ![](images/1a/1a8826befbd05114d1886fbe8fd3f83b2e0bd868bcdedd76f0fd1350451f3bde.svg) is specified with an orientation, we refer to it as a **directed boundary.**\n\nNow, consider the transformation ![](images/0c/0cedb44f040d579cf90349a6891c23cb11a0cc35833979b96447815746831d35.svg) defined by\n\n![](images/a3/a3c2f6f5a3103e4fb023f9f433ce5fa33a215d23f5a33c8bf82b21b852e1236c.svg)\n\nwhere \n\n![](images/d5/d58f2d66646ec6255b8de257d9940476f78ea04fb65a8e0db6d19663399f0efa.svg)\n\nOur task is to find the image of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) under the action of ![](images/43/43a304a55ccd9c3f567bc9ca58aa2fb6b9aa0f8847f74a7c3dbbab3519729bb3.svg) \n\nFirst, note the following:\n\n- It's easy to check that ![](images/ee/eefe57a16ee4b7baeb91597e3e1f25ed4fa583bffef387058ee3d4c563ae7809.svg) has no critical points in the *interior* of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) (we'll discuss why this matters shortly).\n\n- Since ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg)'s interior contains no critical points, we only need to find the image of its boundary ![](images/7d/7d7b652d219e930e1989bd99378c234b82ecbb8aa5eaec0574539267f3f70946.svg)\n\n- To find the image of ![](images/85/85bc8c8e3680298e08b6aecd3c4a66dcfb72f96ef1433d1bccb464af8311aee0.svg) we parametrize each *directed* edge and map them individually onto the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nLet's now map each directed edge of ![](images/1a/1a8826befbd05114d1886fbe8fd3f83b2e0bd868bcdedd76f0fd1350451f3bde.svg) to the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/37/3796e37203fb4ff84036acfaea5f94257c722a75b9b4896885548a54bd1e15ab.svg) where we have ![](images/a9/a983770395cdbf5053f0d2b8e7118ae80bbb9aba8f12def26afe08b3608ca39b.svg) and ![](images/23/233735103d4322c64d03fcb3c3c36b2968737ee86c5932190541afa9b0bf9c58.svg) Substituting ![](images/a9/a983770395cdbf5053f0d2b8e7118ae80bbb9aba8f12def26afe08b3608ca39b.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/26/26d77a4baefb6a2d4a17264f5f8e7b9922e99cdedd62e6bfe2b08aba99ef0d17.svg)\nSo, the image of ![](images/0e/0e765225794b08faeff45f99be1f19d1e37c33b0a0584e6cefa164e0b865d979.svg) can be parametrized by\n![](images/35/3567fd4fae25e9a36d2a48b68dec31311753e8cec3d13b0657db727124eaae3d.svg)\nWriting these parametric equations in the usual notation, we have\n![](images/4f/4fd68f3aee9087b1c0f8b86a57fae2035fa8527d5f0b74038d7c3339e8724457.svg)\nThese are parametric equations of a line segment. Moreover, the endpoints of ![](images/0e/0e765225794b08faeff45f99be1f19d1e37c33b0a0584e6cefa164e0b865d979.svg) are mapped to the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane as follows:\n![](images/46/468ae20a0d581d780de7cb0af719f5a34e3141c44700b0e27353df12cb001bfe.svg)\nTherefore, the image of ![](images/0e/0e765225794b08faeff45f99be1f19d1e37c33b0a0584e6cefa164e0b865d979.svg) is a line segment traversed from the point ![](images/11/11fcd48b1c7ad209409abaa1d91d8c374286782f2f426108d0ef29ee47602ad4.svg) to ![](images/fd/fd83db97b0675d3527a9f2a7a68d38a98f2d05ab5b1771d961c0d687e44c191b.svg)\n\n![](images/13/132642571953ddbfc95c3f0c399cd3c612a2ba2a84a224939b6f40e7d22a074f.png)\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/24/24d3419f793c33397116d086271facafc3e7dc955e696abc2edbd24b307d2d6e.svg) Here, we have ![](images/5d/5d6b28a28a071240c3dc689d764fabcbbc2b3ad5a27491457b455e007260a69d.svg) and ![](images/7c/7cebf80c26d37190278268fc56dc3b471c289f9f9b5112adac1210a972789142.svg) Substituting ![](images/5d/5d6b28a28a071240c3dc689d764fabcbbc2b3ad5a27491457b455e007260a69d.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/41/41118e0a120a0a6df82567ce1925c2b4d21b8f6c50a1c8c927382361d4ab089a.svg)\nThis horizontal line segment is traversed from the point ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\n![](images/13/131644a2410e5127a69388f1bd1fced1e28698e7634b137fcd47f8b3f26121a3.png)\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/c4/c4229f343b9b55013188ec3d2755c4f2b0517098764a1217d19ae440c0a93987.svg) Here, we have ![](images/28/2818d199c13addae115e475e4fd88c1e86f5990ad506cc6193576ec948a5b155.svg) and ![](images/80/8065c8e9bcaad0d0173e6a81ba9da342ceae55c13883b6ae9c9141c595b8bb7f.svg) (we write the inequality for ![](images/46/4620fefc72ebf96810ce9f348c9a728f392246ff87133b6d0e5549607fde421d.svg) in this way to show that ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab00620e798faf115df672253f2eb4a840c7b52610be8.svg) is traversed *from* the point where ![](images/5d/5d6b28a28a071240c3dc689d764fabcbbc2b3ad5a27491457b455e007260a69d.svg) *to* the point where ![](images/b0/b02fba4dd5cb988b97123113f936580578350a063e3c8bcfe498d08b8bdc6fa4.svg)).\n\nSubstituting ![](images/28/2818d199c13addae115e475e4fd88c1e86f5990ad506cc6193576ec948a5b155.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/7e/7ea2885f9400b3908c7dfc98f22b094126c44142421b444b156e44f42e210762.svg)\nSo, the image of ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab00620e798faf115df672253f2eb4a840c7b52610be8.svg) can be parametrized by\n![](images/98/9838e3c4e7e9493167069fcdd079eb2137c6b163497626d42aa6e2283233996e.svg)\nWriting these in the usual notation, we have\n![](images/e4/e4ac0ebf10c9c6d9c69ce2ae73031bf89e3262351a9129fecd6e9225af2d9dd4.svg)\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate ![](images/70/707bb132def3690d81bf1f0c8cf416c1382322ab19d66264cdab1decc4669307.svg) from the equation, we get\n![](images/d4/d488c4edbe9302b258749eb38ec9b104dcaab55c5eb63a88fcff9b00625b6b3b.svg)\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at ![](images/ab/ab6b2f25fb90ef8bea8b21d9d0253752c37bb3c77b675547a223b03f97d3bf6a.svg) Moreover, the endpoints of ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab00620e798faf115df672253f2eb4a840c7b52610be8.svg) are mapped to the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane as follows:\n![](images/05/0537b85fecac45a4548b9907713fb5cbb1bafaeda9f7aa89f712c6e89cb739f8.svg)\nTherefore, the image of ![](images/f4/f4d449c4e8f90acd89fab00620e798faf115df672253f2eb4a840c7b52610be8.svg) is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) to ![](images/ab/ab6b2f25fb90ef8bea8b21d9d0253752c37bb3c77b675547a223b03f97d3bf6a.svg)\n\n![](images/bb/bb73b7c44c9fec45c3c3a632eed03db6f07db3ebbaff9e8e3941d22e78f478db.png)\n\n- Consider the image of the side ![](images/7a/7a8b22dcfbad030cb2ef985aa65b6051afcd75ee410eabdf7e88f931ccb53d81.svg) Here, we have ![](images/b0/b02fba4dd5cb988b97123113f936580578350a063e3c8bcfe498d08b8bdc6fa4.svg) and ![](images/2b/2b2a85d8c09127a2a022c9a63d1c522661922f621a2f3005c877b7c490ca359e.svg) Substituting ![](images/b0/b02fba4dd5cb988b97123113f936580578350a063e3c8bcfe498d08b8bdc6fa4.svg) into our transformation equations, we obtain\n![](images/d6/d62c96cc3ca6e3993cfcf5d5ab2833922e0c6e80689ba8c8dc80e4186ad6cac4.svg) \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point ![](images/dc/dcc19bac01833ff23365244490e52b2722741da56e804e9a95b5b264f23216bf.svg) to the point ![](images/11/11fcd48b1c7ad209409abaa1d91d8c374286782f2f426108d0ef29ee47602ad4.svg) in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\n![](images/7c/7c72a5f4341ab24df69d1375588abd655abbe8fec77ecd41e52501b602b7e3fb.png)\n\n      \n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully computed the image of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane. \n\nIt's highly recommended that you redo this calculation yourself. Make sure you can construct the image of ![](images/13/13e2e6cea8ac23c623cd84a4d1d2ea58e3072ddcc5e8fc720cbe3e91ebff8fbe.svg) including its orientation (note that the image of ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) is traversed *clockwise*)." :tutorial/id #uuid "5c011b18-998e-546d-a4cc-736249313138" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9449 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0183c504-9dca-5d57-b6b2-7fbd845c0c1b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902"} :step/id #uuid "57f1d55f-1700-5d32-8bb5-972acd409176" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158044}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342"} :step/id #uuid "50421ebe-c890-5ba5-a32a-9a94c89dfcb8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa"} :step/id #uuid "a6c347f5-faac-5cfe-bf7c-5e603e73acbf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158046}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158606 :tutorial/content "![](images/74/74e8a3822098768743be16f0af09d4365d424c83ea473eb56160f4c5f15c5531.png)\n\n        \n\nEarlier, we found that the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to \\big( v(u^2+1), \\: u \\big) = \\big( u^2v+v, \\: u \\big) ,\n$$\n\nmaps the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane shown above. \n\nWe deduced the image of $\\Delta$ by mapping each edge of its boundary. For this method to work, we need to check that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the *interior* of $\\Delta.$ \n\nTo check for critical points, we start by computing the Jacobian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFor the partial derivatives of $x(u,v),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}  = \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial u}(u^2v+v) = 2uv, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial v} =  \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial v}(u^2v+v) = u^2+1,\n$$\n\nand for the partial derivatives of $y(u,v),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}  = \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial u}(u) = 1, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}  = \\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial v}(u) = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our partial derivatives into our expression for the Jacobian, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2uv & {u}^{2}+1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(1+{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- The Jacobian is negative everywhere in the $(u,v)$ plane. So, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n- Since the Jacobian is negative everywhere, the transformation $\\mathbf T$ is orientation-reversing everywhere (note, for example, that $\\Delta$ and $D$ have opposite orientation).\n\nTo apply this method, we require that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the *interior* of $\\Delta.$ This method works even if the *boundary* of $\\Delta$ contains critical points. Let's explore this a little more." :tutorial/id #uuid "40f68814-34bc-5d92-b1f0-78ce6045757d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9450 :tutorial/title "The Significance of Critical Points"} :step/id #uuid "f217b585-996f-5193-9dbe-40fa68d75ee1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158607 :tutorial/content "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's again consider our square region $\\Delta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nNow consider the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to \\big( uv, \\: u^2+uv) \\big).\n$$\n\nThe Jacobian matrix determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 2u+v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSetting the Jacobian equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n-2u^2 = 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf{T}$ lie on the line $u = 0.$ This coincides with the side $S_4$ of our square.\n\n![](images/6e/6ea454e2a95bf5a9bef1d585c1620ff78a98586c2500293cdfd136c7562a5f83.png)\n\n  \n\nNow, since there are no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta,$ we can find its image in the usual way. However, the side $S_4$ may collapse to a single point. So, let's go ahead and finish this example." :tutorial/id #uuid "af247fbf-2e40-5486-964b-7b1effb2c06f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9451 :tutorial/title "Critical Points on the Boundary of the Domain"} :step/id #uuid "39d12d5d-e40b-5a0a-9a55-6c687ac4c3b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056"} :step/id #uuid "7ca8a633-49fc-5b82-89ef-24ced4348ee4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158608 :tutorial/content "Until now, we've specified that our transformation $\\mathbf T$ should not contain critical points in the interior of the preimage $\\Delta.$ So let's briefly discuss what to do when $\\Delta$ contains critical points.\n\nOnce again, let's consider the region \n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nin the $uv$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n             \n\nWe now consider the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to \\big( uv, \\: u^2+v^2 \\big).\n$$\n\nLet's start by finding the critical points. First, we compute the Jacobian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{v}^{2}-2{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2(v+u)(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe critical points correspond to the solutions of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x,y)}{\\partial (u,v)} = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points are given by\n\n$$\n2(v+u)(v-u) = 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u=\\pm v.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf T$ that lie *inside* $\\Delta$ are located on the line $u = v.$\n\n![](images/af/af0cb392b9194a37c7f7e29455e066be52b28e11c8486edc8c992a82d3df625a.png)\n\n             \n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ in this case, we divide $\\Delta$ into two regions $\\Delta_1$ and $\\Delta_2$ along the critical line (as shown below) and compute their images separately.\n\n![](images/96/965c24ef46882d40d4ff71b30b2ff60f891298a5dc48a267afc946f719e2b245.png)\n\n     \n\nLet's compute the images of these two regions:\n\n- Computing the image of $\\Delta_1$'s boundary, we find that \n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_1)$ is a vertical line segment traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1),$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_2)$ is an upward-opening parabola traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,2),$ and\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(C_1)$ is a line segment traversed from $(1,2)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](images/c7/c79e3dfcc15a6f5f7c77c59e99d67a29103e77a550a7c53a98b6868d786f5925.png)\n\n     \n\n- Similarly, computing the image of $\\Delta_2$'s boundary, we find that \n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(C_2),$ a line segment traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2),$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_3),$ an upward-opening parabola traversed from $(1,2)$ to $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(S_4),$ a vertical line segment traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](images/eb/ebeae2b6425c15fb5670fc83dc64f07a2d407fd108d26c51d8f301d979732cdb.png)\n\n     \n\nSuperimposing the two results, we obtain the following image of $\\Delta{:}$\n\n![](images/28/28993d9cff3621f5b1633940c161aa528ef2b143bafca967cb55bdd9ffa4e1e0.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- As $(u,v)$ runs over $\\Delta$ once, its image $D$ is traced out twice.\n\n- The boundary of $D$ that runs from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2)$ is the image of the critical line $u=v,$ which is *not* a bounding curve of our original region $\\Delta.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8817e0ca-4c4f-5e82-b97a-2f4e6047eab4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9452 :tutorial/title "Critical Points Inside the Domain"} :step/id #uuid "658b558d-159e-5930-8d10-145b5fecb379" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158609 :tutorial/content "We'll now justify why the Jacobian determinant gives the (local) area scale factor of a transformation.\n\nConsider a rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane of length $\\delta u,$ width $\\delta v,$ whose bottom-left corner is at the point $(u_0,v_0),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/9a/9a7d700c343fd2807e70b43d688c2f47ed3724e88fc9849e23aa20ee0be10b15.png)\n\nFurthermore, we assume the following:\n\n- $\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow \\mathbb R^2$ is a $C^1$ transformation throughout $\\Delta,$ and contains no critical points.\n\n- The image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the region $D.$\n\n- The image of $(u_0,v_0)$ is $(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- The image of $S_1$ is $S_1',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $A.$\n\n- The image of $S_2$ is $S_2',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $B.$\n\nSince $S_1'$ is the image of $S_1,$ it can be parametrized by varying $u$ only. Similarly, since $S_2'$ is the image of $S_2,$ it can be parametrized by varying $v$ only. So, the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are given by\n\n![](images/1b/1be386cd85ad48bcc75b4d292ebeeee0d8af0bf8df2297696b9384c448130bb3.svg)\n\nNow, since $\\delta u$ is small, $S_1'$ can be approximated by a line segment. Similarly, since $\\delta v$ is small, $S_2'$ can also be approximated by a line segment. Let's construct two vectors $\\mathbf{s}_1$ and $\\mathbf{s}_2$ that span our two segments, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1ff10cdcde237698a28c6f76053de50745ef12c67dfc5290ac6e06893d201972.png)\n\nWe can find the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ by subtracting the position vectors of its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1}=a-\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that when $\\delta u$ is small, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{∂x}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u} \\\\ {\\frac{∂y}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ can be approximated as follows:\n\n![](images/b7/b766b50b744af71539daaf12c1e32e44f493ac71bbabb8b387c78bb8092886b8.svg)\n\nUsing similar arguments, we have the following approximation for $\\mathbf s_2{:}$\n\n![](images/f3/f3203536e63ab67521014fe9e8d623cf00535ae8d35adee9b6405253d532dcfc.svg)\n\nThe area of $D$ is approximately equal to the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2.$ Therefore,\n\n![](images/55/55a8a36b0617400f963430eb1ba31ef20cbddeb4f09eaeaa8920fae39b4b145e.svg)\n\nSubstituting our approximations for $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2,$ we arrive at the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ≈{|det\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\,\\,\\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & ={|\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot (\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\frac{∂y}{∂u})|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & =\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot {|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "07200f56-2408-51a2-9e3d-6b5d727ef5ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9453 :tutorial/title "The Jacobian as a Local Area Scale Factor"} :step/id #uuid "69d5e524-4c34-5148-a189-6502b171d912"}] :activity/title "Nonlinear Transformations of Plane Regions" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157488} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416675} :activity/id #uuid "23a36746-618b-5408-8fcd-c638b93c9f28" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416670 :question/id #uuid "b9cdc646-22ad-53d7-894a-78ecf8a59507"} :step/id #uuid "283e81ba-165c-5712-b058-a61afb12269f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416669 :question/id #uuid "aedbf0c6-276b-509d-933d-5c28129e8de2"} :step/id #uuid "526a1df8-1606-5e36-8deb-549a340f512b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416674}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416673 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290425 :question/id #uuid "804dcc9f-4962-5beb-bc3d-f0bb08f62de0"} :step/id #uuid "ba6262b9-b0fe-5e23-a4c4-b3dd83e994ec"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290422 :question/id #uuid "98c6216e-4e18-5967-be2c-62a3b8d14d71"} :step/id #uuid "bc6691b7-166a-5089-867e-8ee8a5dc5783" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416673}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389063 :question/id #uuid "5e3c0bea-5d6e-5a25-bcb3-abdcd207f44f"} :step/id #uuid "c4aa2659-822f-5c81-9961-6d81d19483c6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416671}}] :activity/title "Nonlinear Transformations of Plane Regions" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416656} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436392} :activity/id #uuid "5d1b8939-e94e-5773-a283-f5d685750aa5" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436389 :question/id #uuid "015f5e64-8ccf-5a6a-8fbf-8ce2c8d20eda"} :step/id #uuid "01e6ff8f-00df-5949-ac6e-0410dc19e1c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436391}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373902 :question/id #uuid "e4de9b2d-c42a-5fe3-94b3-1b2caac795c3"} :step/id #uuid "1a287a6a-b990-525b-8eed-0161adc076ba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436393}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157614 :question/id #uuid "13842938-0c5a-56c1-b7e1-75af1a627153"} :step/id #uuid "8a728ed1-bdb3-5d79-95ad-38502010151b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436394}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290419 :question/id #uuid "13002a7e-2ad6-501a-b8db-7a8abc685d5d"} :step/id #uuid "c2c63c2f-25ab-55d2-98c6-3bc6cfeeba08"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157615 :question/id #uuid "5ebd6f32-1c51-561f-a98e-5d832c9928ee"} :step/id #uuid "fd5f8cf2-cf21-51d5-98dd-cd133c6f5651" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436390}}] :activity/title "Nonlinear Transformations of Plane Regions" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436377}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049373 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157246 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157028 :question/id #uuid "ec73a2b5-8f4a-5418-983d-b35013387844" :question/math-academy-id "e-15087" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = u - v^2, \\qquad y = 2v.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side $S_4$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{x(t)}, \\qquad y=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_4.$\n\nFor the side $S_4,$ we have $u = 0$ and $1 \\geq v \\geq 0.$ Substituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-{v}^{2},\\,2v) \\\\  & =(0-{v}^{2},\\,2v) \\\\  & =(-{v}^{2},\\,2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_4$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = -v^2, \\qquad y=2v, \\qquad 1 \\geq v \\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-t^2}, \\qquad y(t)=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00826534-9ddb-597e-a5b4-4a93def9bd22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407179} {:answer/id #uuid "1402d9d4-6fb9-5711-a369-724d5ea71016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407181} {:answer/id #uuid "924df19c-bb6c-5527-89a8-97aeec9e95f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407192} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c147ca-5893-5b85-a3fd-b0d1b30cb874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407194} {:answer/id #uuid "e270c150-b9e2-53a0-a3c8-c86305ab1238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407192} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c2bcbff-1030-59ca-9fb9-26027999b654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407208}] :question/id #uuid "8b1e6ffe-be0a-5264-9741-046fc4e7d069" :question/math-academy-id "q-160357" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=u(1+{v}^{2}).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=y(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the function $y(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) \\\\  & =(v,\\,1(1+{v}^{2})) \\\\  & =(v,1+{v}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=1+{v}^{2},\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=1+{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38802a43-225d-5220-a08b-5d8987ac8767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275978} {:answer/id #uuid "5336c3df-6190-55c6-819b-810587107049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276215} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8efc7a-1a6e-5166-afd8-cad0a5311d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277147} {:answer/id #uuid "e14f1faf-2067-5875-9127-c6e39fa6db9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277446} {:answer/id #uuid "fee3ec3b-c828-590b-aa18-c5c24d0c43f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277147} :answer-field/id #uuid "91880f3e-ce37-5e5b-aabf-8ab49052c15b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277982}] :question/id #uuid "c81115ea-cfb5-58ec-bdf5-a51e72b6004d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160605" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u(1+v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=y(t),\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $y(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-v,\\,u(1+v)) \\\\  & =(0-v,\\,0(1+v)) \\\\  & =(-v,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-v,\\,y=0,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-t,\\,y(t)=0,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e672f1-86d5-5fed-8e62-a0b1f7417a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275661} {:answer/id #uuid "2a39bed3-e507-5b75-8ecd-b8cc12ff9912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275858} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6bc04f-385a-5567-a055-49babe18c830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275863} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee19309-a9c7-5394-866d-036f139c441b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276181} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2966ff-90e9-5471-926b-a40de85a3a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276754} :answer-field/id #uuid "abae6f9e-7c68-59b1-ba20-d96a148ece8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278021}] :question/id #uuid "ad2c8de4-8a2b-5de9-ac83-ed83d956c1b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160602" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,y=u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,u) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{0}^{2},\\,u) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "173fe1e1-8a96-5c65-89e8-cb02e4edb4b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275696} {:answer/id #uuid "2545c0c7-9c02-5fc2-b641-7a81fd86dff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275815} {:answer/id #uuid "9757f319-5d0f-5372-8bf7-922616f4343c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276838} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f558a8-f159-54f7-b6ad-1df020c8cd6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277331} {:answer/id #uuid "ea47b49f-7450-5cca-a2cd-29ee07630e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275815} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f05e03f-d312-501e-ba3e-4eddc1ad9cdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277756}] :question/id #uuid "fbc1cc5f-b781-5ba3-8388-72105273d004" :question/math-academy-id "q-160604" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={v}^{2}-u,\\,y=uv.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({v}^{2}-u,\\,uv) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-u,\\,u(1)) \\\\  & =(1-u,\\,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1-u,\\,y=u,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1-t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b62c6d5-bc56-5614-8275-68db85558f23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288443} {:answer/id #uuid "61af5008-f855-5b19-a1ca-7d1e3ad76e89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288718} {:answer/id #uuid "7485208e-4e8f-5fd1-b844-05cfb7d1e85c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288842} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2510ca-94f6-57fb-bc21-fa8fa2c0fa5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289084} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d02c9c-eccf-50cf-91ff-41f7ac11c841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289084} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3e046e1-7044-56f5-8d75-cfee2510c0eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290098}] :question/id #uuid "3ff738bf-e8fd-5476-844b-109db4ff56ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-225021" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v-u,\\,y=u(u+v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=1+t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v-u,\\,u(u+v)) \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,1(1+v)) \\\\  & =(v-1,1+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v-1,\\,y=1+v,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)=1+t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "793fda82-4b8f-5357-8355-7eb714c2f363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401896} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a3c59d-ed02-5238-a8c6-fd2024482e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401897} {:answer/id #uuid "f6cd580d-4800-5b0c-92e0-e1600068f58e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401898} {:answer/id #uuid "b627bbcd-b10a-5d39-a559-125ce37018ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401899} {:answer/id #uuid "ee753101-7bb5-5dbb-a4a6-c54e0820154f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401897} :answer-field/id #uuid "618ade45-7715-5c2b-8e23-5edac27f8883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401895}] :question/id #uuid "6706b3e3-ba1f-5e48-a0c6-87c0de600ccd" :question/math-academy-id "q-225023" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v(2-u),\\,y=u{v}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $1\\ge u\\ge 0$ and $v=1.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v(2-u),\\,u{v}^{3}) \\\\  & =(1(2-u),\\,u(1)^{3}) \\\\  & =(2-u,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2-u,\\,y=u,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2-t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2dcac8d-e192-52d2-a860-f232f64707c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407195} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1c4bec-d2d1-5ebd-aec5-afc647b5dca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407196} {:answer/id #uuid "dc27fb6b-147f-5701-a343-70552c33d5c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407199} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7efbbb-13d6-52d4-bb72-add345cc6fa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407200} {:answer/id #uuid "e504c718-1387-5ae2-8eea-bdebd385c5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407196} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aab39e5-3823-5bb8-b773-c98978518f2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407205}] :question/id #uuid "d45fe3c2-157b-597a-b7a4-36ff992f60f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-225018" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v+2u,\\,y=u({v}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,y=y(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the function $y(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $v=0.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v+2u,\\,u({v}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =(0+2u,\\,u(0-1)) \\\\  & =(2u,-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u,\\,y=-u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2t,\\,y(t)=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157247 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157027 :question/id #uuid "f35926af-6c5f-537f-ab22-ec2d62995267" :question/math-academy-id "e-15066" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ and its **directed** boundary $\\partial\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, where $\\Delta$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = 2u-3v, \\qquad y=v^2-3u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side $S_3$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_3.$\n\nFor the side $S_3,$ we have $v=1$ and $1 \\geq u \\geq 0.$ Substituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2u-3v,\\,{v}^{2}-3u) \\\\  & =(2u-3(1),\\,{1}^{2}-3u) \\\\  & =(2u-3,1-3u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_3$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u-3, \\qquad y=1-3u, \\qquad 1 \\geq u \\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = 2t-3, \\qquad y(t) = 1-3t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-2)$ and $(-3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/15/1583f8d3ad69b8d5e3ac051eeb50b55860737a60d462d126ae5dc2b49c3ea155.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8f59d19-7a8a-59b1-8f18-b6c96dc5f5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 0)$ and $(1, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153911} {:answer/id #uuid "e54ecd5a-f515-54b0-83dc-5b23c4c16ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 0)$ and $(0, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153912} {:answer/id #uuid "04db1c4b-a2d5-5bf1-a2ff-fd4a5f9705cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 0)$ and $(0, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153913} {:answer/id #uuid "30c1dbb0-fcc9-58f1-89f0-3d17989e833f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 0)$ and $(1, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153914} {:answer/id #uuid "53699c26-264c-52da-8599-f62f8377efc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 1)$ and $(0, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153915}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b1096e20-dce5-528e-95e9-fe575b110cc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153910}] :question/id #uuid "5ed90667-761a-5947-8a5e-203a983f722a" :question/math-academy-id "q-160478" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = v, y = u(1 + v^{2})$.\n\nThe image of the side $S_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b4e018a-bce1-5b10-a192-2b9d54af71ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436379} {:answer/id #uuid "57862c3c-92cb-58ef-989f-3796b1f3d3f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436380} {:answer/id #uuid "809bfe03-d49c-5afa-b88d-848b85922201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436382} {:answer/id #uuid "8166063a-dc78-5f3f-8bf7-0c141a4e8d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436383} {:answer/id #uuid "96d545bf-0741-5454-b677-38f6a4355e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436383} :answer-field/id #uuid "220de041-8a77-59e5-81bf-784ea13caa4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153928}] :question/id #uuid "13842938-0c5a-56c1-b7e1-75af1a627153" :question/math-academy-id "q-160610" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=u-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v,\\,u-{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1,\\,u-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1,\\,u-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=u-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1,\\,y(t)=t-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment with endpoints $(1,0)$ and $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/4c/4cda505c17437f64f8ab90dcccca1f55f087af77be6dee0e7c81dbd372760664.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a303dc-f918-50e3-813e-224b2d5ada75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288037} {:answer/id #uuid "19cd60b3-89d9-5b97-ba07-e247159970b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288197} {:answer/id #uuid "6040ba21-9a76-5266-a8cc-6d6a36605c72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288704} {:answer/id #uuid "8548c197-3752-5a10-9f54-e93e5066160c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288932} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5a7403-cdae-5fd1-9063-fa942b91c2fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288932} :answer-field/id #uuid "02414deb-2129-5a8c-92f0-e0e35d8fc56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289732}] :question/id #uuid "b39b2a59-ee1b-58ec-89c3-bea4b6d6a4dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-225035" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v-u,\\,y={v}^{3}+u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v-u,\\,{v}^{3}+u) \\\\  & =(0-u,\\,{0}^{3}+u) \\\\  & =(-u,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-u,\\,y=u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(-1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/fc/fc5daa3ee483ed2a7e78d30a247c6d4c9ae4d77691d5d5659c0b9ac2fdf6621a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7e3316-44a4-51dd-88ec-54b6759ae285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288207} {:answer/id #uuid "41e8768d-a388-5f24-80e5-419c2d1e308a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288481} {:answer/id #uuid "4887cd2e-4dc8-552d-8323-351995a2d97e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288535} {:answer/id #uuid "cc545379-ca98-54da-a641-03165a1e8074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289391} {:answer/id #uuid "dc575b8a-4471-5b2d-b642-d6f458c81ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288535} :answer-field/id #uuid "5baa911f-9455-5868-a1c9-400d786bbf8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289925}] :question/id #uuid "0db3ac95-ce09-5363-b03f-51fa8a776210" :question/math-academy-id "q-225036" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u-{v}^{2},\\,y=2(u-v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-{v}^{2},\\,2(u-v)) \\\\  & =(u-{0}^{2},\\,2(u-0)) \\\\  & =(u,2u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y=2u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9d/9d9c8e967d4290f0a75e071762ff05c97b12367f43f8a1a965fff09efa64743b.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0955cb65-e675-5058-9322-f852b34e73ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288103} {:answer/id #uuid "168bab5a-b1a5-5cca-bfc0-b2b50f0e0d98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,1)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288183} {:answer/id #uuid "249c379c-9294-51f3-a99b-eadafe2f08c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288267} {:answer/id #uuid "345fe46f-0e5f-5ee6-874f-c5903cef110d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288378} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4a0139-b09c-5a14-8d39-203a8313901b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288267} :answer-field/id #uuid "8520da6e-99c2-5304-82a0-e751b55fd1d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290023}] :question/id #uuid "18700a7b-fa8a-5875-8b98-b10f04141777" :question/math-academy-id "q-225042" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={v}^{2}+2u,\\,y=v-u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({v}^{2}+2u,\\,v-u) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+2u,\\,1-u) \\\\  & =(1+2u,\\,1-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1+2u,\\,y=1-u,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1+2t,\\,y(t)=1-t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(3,0)$ and $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/f7/f75f8beceedef7d64d12c7cf1d4bc838039ecdeae3de752c45e76975f116fcb2.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04269a37-ef4d-59a8-80a1-90325d918f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288060} {:answer/id #uuid "45c65eab-be4e-5eec-afc1-d8fd72b902fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288505} {:answer/id #uuid "58d2ee34-b79b-50db-8e98-66429e58a21b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1)$ and $(0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288653} {:answer/id #uuid "6069cd3e-3414-5044-8473-a4a78c20e6bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288706} {:answer/id #uuid "e04f5cc1-67bc-5116-bd79-3108b41506f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1)$ and $(0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288653} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ae61c12-3643-5cfa-aa25-25c5703b9ee2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289754}] :question/id #uuid "87562d4b-d2dd-5a36-afa0-f983f6ea5c37" :question/math-academy-id "q-225046" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=(u-1)v,\\,y=uv-1.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =((u-1)v,\\,uv-1) \\\\  & =((0-1)v,\\,(0)v-1) \\\\  & =(-v,-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-v,\\,y=-1,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-t,\\,y(t)=-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment with endpoints $(-1,-1)$ and $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/48/48ae89e14005e5fc21f0693e769e6214c1c9a94ba0cd63e7ce08d9771fbf82f1.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "780de9d9-e4b5-5781-8cef-3b27427c5453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(0,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288858} {:answer/id #uuid "8a67c6ed-e594-58da-a3e2-9cde9e2eeccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(-2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288959} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac74926-fbfa-5370-84f1-617228903423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289069} {:answer/id #uuid "da43167a-eb1e-579e-9489-263a50362529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289488} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f652a0-c78e-5c7a-80a1-75956fd274ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289069} :answer-field/id #uuid "059ab4f0-a446-568d-b776-8d45b78e5521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289740}] :question/id #uuid "d4936677-eae4-5d50-a274-69a0a7b683b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-225119" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+4v,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(v-4u).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u+4v,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(v-4u)) \\\\  & =(u+4(0),\\,\\frac{1}{2}(0-4u)) \\\\  & =(u,-2u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y=-2u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=-2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(1,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9a/9af81ffe277395ce00ef597235bb8f440ff22e5da9db5cbe3266dae8ba0fe281.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06977182-5497-5c11-a57f-add4aeca5aa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288084} {:answer/id #uuid "2ece2530-2ca7-5ec1-a8cf-c55ac6d46052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288347} {:answer/id #uuid "8539bd62-399f-5793-bd3f-e4de85fa29cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288928} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d93a8c-2868-5548-992e-86797465c0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289208} {:answer/id #uuid "bdbf2fce-7275-51db-ae2f-706d92628bd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-1)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288347} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cf66fd9-3498-5637-8fc1-454524321dda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289928}] :question/id #uuid "13002a7e-2ad6-501a-b8db-7a8abc685d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-225122" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}(v+6u),\\,y=\\frac{{v}^{3}-2u}{v+2}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}(v+6u),\\,\\frac{{v}^{3}-2u}{v+2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}(0+6u),\\,\\frac{{0}^{3}-2u}{0+2}) \\\\  & =(2u,-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u,\\,y=-u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2t,\\,y(t)=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(2,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/f0/f068883d1318acdafba39c74bc1279f6cafe803437a9b79249999df461bac8ee.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01dc1235-23a0-5a65-9aaa-39fbcc88d423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288040} {:answer/id #uuid "157ecca2-a3da-529c-adac-05ef42f6f50a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288178} {:answer/id #uuid "401ef9eb-4ef9-58bc-9a72-dbce5cc69aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288469} {:answer/id #uuid "6138ed2c-0da4-5f4f-b3fd-e71fb6d30c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288712} {:answer/id #uuid "85965dab-709d-5792-a99d-026740350c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(-2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288040} :answer-field/id #uuid "902adaae-f208-5162-9a04-66fd5676fafe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290051}] :question/id #uuid "f73a9fb4-f498-52df-9232-bbaac05c0c5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-225150" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=2v(u-1),\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}v(4-u).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2v(u-1),\\,\\frac{1}{2}v(4-u)) \\\\  & =(2v(0-1),\\,\\frac{1}{2}v(4-0)) \\\\  & =(-2v,2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-2v,\\,y=2v,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-2t,\\,y(t)=2t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/e6/e6ef0f52fbe6638659dd7b7f3d702d4bfbb07b83a7f7742c9b0822157908b706.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38b93a84-d633-56d5-acf0-64e865f690ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401195} {:answer/id #uuid "a58ae1f1-f6a6-59d0-9751-fca06c5ac749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401196} {:answer/id #uuid "062653e1-93bc-5e7e-a802-152e2de8a4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401197} {:answer/id #uuid "d40073c8-1340-52d4-b3a8-b1c6162defe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401198} {:answer/id #uuid "37e2e5c1-32d5-5a19-92b2-99790aa5b623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401197} :answer-field/id #uuid "1398b2db-d522-591e-9af9-65f757fcf9ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401194}] :question/id #uuid "5f849a8c-d5a9-5291-8dbf-bee7c6e87ffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160608" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v-{u}^{2},\\,y=u+v.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v-{u}^{2},\\,u+v) \\\\  & =(v-{1}^{2},\\,1+v) \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,v+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v-1,\\,y=v+1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)=t+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,1)$ and $(0,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/f3/f304c409cfc62de3d296690c0acd2cec5b9726c03d8a196ed69de36d8ebbefd2.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e26268d-49a1-5a83-8429-e2dbae2639b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407182} {:answer/id #uuid "5165e145-830c-56a0-9173-fcfbf2800e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407187} {:answer/id #uuid "524c3a00-a206-5dde-a568-5a05da724097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407188} {:answer/id #uuid "87c16c9a-0fa3-5130-8d05-8c94f59b0ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407191} {:answer/id #uuid "b16f1a1f-9a49-5973-9be2-b7ebcc061568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407193} :answer-field/id #uuid "07fdf1dc-3a9e-518a-9121-2ac6a2492963" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407204}] :question/id #uuid "5c666819-f94c-5831-9c13-86415b44c537" :question/math-academy-id "q-225047" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+2v,\\,y=v-2u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+2v,\\,v-2u) \\\\  & =({0}^{2}+2v,\\,v-2(0)) \\\\  & =(2v,v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2v,\\,y=v,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(2,1)$ and $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/0b/0b61f3a91c912dcd042bdd5cc11b0f393a8ea85269ecbef000403cf080166cf7.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31e82cf1-a965-5d27-8faf-45515b67a2ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416657} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9762f5-d942-5b02-90e1-433937835992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416658} {:answer/id #uuid "758ca0b2-013b-5d8c-a6a5-3ac1ce65e6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-1)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416662} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7d123e-9efa-5d77-821b-8c45d6d2e441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-1)$ and $(3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416663} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c04fe0-8935-5aff-a0db-580177fae6a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416666} :answer-field/id #uuid "d47620cc-ff04-5478-975c-18645160652a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416668}] :question/id #uuid "aedbf0c6-276b-509d-933d-5c28129e8de2" :question/math-academy-id "q-225139" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=2{v}^{2}+u,\\,y=u-{v}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2{v}^{2}+u,\\,u-{v}^{4}) \\\\  & =(2(1)^{2}+u,\\,u-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =(2+u,\\,u-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2+u,\\,y=u-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2+t,\\,y(t)=t-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(3,0)$ and $(2,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/2a/2ac0e0eac9e4633a8d29881782d8dfe93c4c594ca0f99912a98507822c97c4b4.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157248 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157025 :question/id #uuid "bb5306eb-02c5-52a3-b7b0-92610059b065" :question/math-academy-id "e-15064" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = ( u + v)^2, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{u}{v+1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side $S_1$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_1.$\n\nFor the side $S_1,$ we have $v = 0$ and $0 \\leq u \\leq 1.$ Substituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =((u+v)^{2},\\,\\frac{u}{v+1}) \\\\  & =((u+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{u}{0+1}) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_1$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = u^2, \\qquad y = u, \\qquad 0 \\leq u \\leq 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^2, \\qquad y(t) = t, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny^2=x\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of $S_1$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to ((0+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{0}{0+1})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to ((1+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{0+1})=(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $S_1$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$\n\n![](images/7f/7f94f6fef873efd64d9da9e015e0056944b63846d741e12937507205b118638b.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc68d80-39d1-5f0f-933c-89bd1b4b4d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373897} {:answer/id #uuid "b20916f4-2125-5e54-be30-7b22ca6d6f51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(2,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373898} {:answer/id #uuid "f69006ab-7ec1-5771-bd33-a19fe8da912c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,-1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373899} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3fa41a-6316-5747-87cf-e13860d909ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,-1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373900} {:answer/id #uuid "732254b6-7d89-598e-b5b3-79f480fa4f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373897} :answer-field/id #uuid "419acef2-b0e8-560d-bfb9-5d30aad77e63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373896}] :question/id #uuid "5ebd6f32-1c51-561f-a98e-5d832c9928ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-160611" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y=-v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,-v) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,-v) \\\\  & =({v}^{2}+1,\\,-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={v}^{2}+1,\\,y=-v,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+1,\\,y=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=(-y)^{2}+1\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=x-1\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(1,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{2}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,0)\\to ({1}^{2}+{0}^{2},-0)=(1,0) \\\\  & T:(1,1)\\to ({1}^{2}+{1}^{2},-1)=(2,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{2}$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-1).$\n\n![](images/ad/ade48b3f14ed83460a5d818f744103edd75f8394a3f3396a0ff30364bda36c9b.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b56a8130-4490-5f57-ac24-06945f035969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1, 1)$ to $(2, 1)$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154568} {:answer/id #uuid "e5bfd3da-8e27-5473-b001-141883f8d3da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2, 0)$ to $(0, 0)$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154569} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ce9a77-a490-5f42-8785-bf2fa7142df4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0, 0)$ to $(2, 0)$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154570} {:answer/id #uuid "2d655c3a-9c7f-5a44-978c-e0dea412c645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0, 0)$ to $(0, 2)$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154571} {:answer/id #uuid "36992775-f441-55f7-b0c1-6fcb4704f738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0, 2)$ to $(0, 0)$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154572}] :answer-field/id #uuid "233d554e-4ad1-5b50-b52f-6f5348d99c04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c88fa794-c497-5f52-9270-c4f70ba34b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(2,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374546} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe56a20-a72e-5a8f-b8ae-bfbf738e2e84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,2)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374547} {:answer/id #uuid "23baad64-0f91-56a2-94bb-91156bdfc995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(2,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374548} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0203aa-f7bb-595d-81ac-41e80db5854c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374549} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5eef94-634a-55b7-ba52-fefb80987128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,0)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,0)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374550} :answer-field/id #uuid "83b393c7-e9cc-5ebd-b02d-4619e5776017" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374545}] :question/id #uuid "9ac0dde1-ceb6-5217-a457-3b5e7f5732af" :question/math-academy-id "q-225164" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u(v+1),\\,y=4u(v-u).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u(v+1),\\,4u(v-u)) \\\\  & =(u(1+1),\\,4u(1-u)) \\\\  & =(2u,\\,4u(1-u)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u,\\,y=4u(1-u),\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2t,\\,y(t)=4t(1-t),\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny=2x(1-\\frac{x}{2})\\,⇒\\,y=-(x-1)^{2}+1\n$$\n\nwhich is a downward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(1,1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{3}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,1)\\to ((1)(1+1),\\,4(1)(1-1))=(2,0) \\\\  & T:(0,1)\\to ((0)(1+1),\\,4(0)(1-0))=(0,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{3}$ is an arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,0)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](images/97/970003245107516d9be7b4729d6c18abf01d1f48c82211402f06d13cec4e3a08.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7985e0-0857-5804-a91d-6874ee7f39a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275582} {:answer/id #uuid "34415490-023d-5c18-bba7-038451023e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275941} {:answer/id #uuid "6e076705-7458-5ed0-8526-2a222bbe61f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276461} {:answer/id #uuid "d588ca3a-96a4-544f-90a5-ba156834c738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,2)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277358} {:answer/id #uuid "f885127e-36b3-5abe-b21f-1dd3b715c6b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from  $(1,0)$ to $(2,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275582} :answer-field/id #uuid "eefec963-55e6-5c35-9531-076352440923" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278147}] :question/id #uuid "235488a1-420f-5270-8713-f985503b0b2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160607" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u(u+v),\\,y=\\frac{2u}{v+1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u(u+v),\\,\\frac{2u}{v+1}) \\\\  & =(u(u+0),\\,\\frac{2u}{0+1}) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,2u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=2u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)=2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx={(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=4x\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{1}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to (0(0+0),\\,\\frac{2(0)}{0+1})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to (1(1+0),\\,\\frac{2(1)}{0+1})=(1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{1}$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$\n\n![](images/4f/4fc3c209e83fc1d6b4c9258cdb739fb0a73db797791cf8fcca2e51a7592d54f8.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c33fd05-3cfb-58f5-9254-c294b979fb88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288121} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8a2f00-5bc0-5fa4-9944-3c47fed8b977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from  $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288351} {:answer/id #uuid "3dee0dcd-5173-521d-91ee-c163feaf0aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288456} {:answer/id #uuid "5914bae7-7c14-53ae-a38b-25c683ec186f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288655} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3abc26-6d08-5f7d-b214-f9184ec127af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289504} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd4d0632-afac-503f-8c6c-4b97e5ca7fa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290177}] :question/id #uuid "a5b81ca6-81f2-5af0-8e47-706a9cf0ae51" :question/math-academy-id "q-225158" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u(u-{v}^{3}).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-v,\\,u(u-{v}^{3})) \\\\  & =(u-0,\\,u(u-{0}^{3})) \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y={u}^{2},\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)={t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=y\\,⇒\\,y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{1}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to (0-0,\\,0(0-{0}^{3}))=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to (1-0,\\,1(1-{0}^{3}))=(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{1}$ is an arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$\n\n![](images/7a/7aa56f8bba41a352a02c6d26554c417e7844cd60c28078da51cbf16a3220e57d.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61988fc9-66c2-5bcc-83c1-08b33f8bfdfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288717} {:answer/id #uuid "73ab294c-d61b-5d99-813d-6afefa5b3b32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288837} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ad950e-b09a-59c9-8006-5f0ea759fde8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289112} {:answer/id #uuid "a532b5c7-b1d3-5762-99e5-5ee19c94f559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(2,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289131} {:answer/id #uuid "ba18cd8c-2285-5486-8226-bfb496f3b81a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,1)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289112} :answer-field/id #uuid "e191ff6e-214b-558e-9919-97349fa4da47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290210}] :question/id #uuid "98c6216e-4e18-5967-be2c-62a3b8d14d71" :question/math-academy-id "q-225160" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,y=1-u+v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,1-u+v) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,1-1+v) \\\\  & =(1-{v}^{2},\\,v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1-{v}^{2},\\,y=v,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=1-{t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=1-{y}^{2}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=1-x\n$$\n\nwhich is a left-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(1,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{2}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,0)\\to ({1}^{2}-{0}^{2},1-1+0)=(1,0) \\\\  & T:(1,1)\\to ({1}^{2}-{1}^{0},1-1+1)=(0,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{2}$ is an arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$\n\n![](images/99/995b106a25173b4e3667fd3fce04f499d7d4a549ae76414328f6e3c406ddd3c6.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e0441d-7176-5665-92dd-c26fbcb98df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288030} {:answer/id #uuid "09d94921-07ab-5e03-a01d-a08146f1581b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288110} {:answer/id #uuid "6fad7b2e-173a-553d-a0c3-c112c2827a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288807} {:answer/id #uuid "d6fc6f2a-51d8-5a81-9055-de9f6ce7af3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289461} {:answer/id #uuid "efe4bf53-696a-5f8e-b619-8486266ab583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288030} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4b747c-3e97-5b8d-86b0-f905f174bf6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289966}] :question/id #uuid "21fdd56a-1bd5-5736-b8c0-5f4753ddc47e" :question/math-academy-id "q-225166" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=2v(1-u-v),\\,y=\\frac{1-v}{1+u}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2v(1-u-v),\\,\\frac{1-v}{1+u}) \\\\  & =(2v(1-0-v),\\,\\frac{1-v}{1+0}) \\\\  & =(2v(1-v),\\,1-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2v(1-v),\\,y=1-v,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=2t(1-t),\\,y=1-t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=2(1-y)(y)\\,⇒\\,x=-2{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a left-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (2(1)(1-0-1),\\frac{1-1}{1+0})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (2(0)(1-0-0),\\frac{1-0}{1+0})=(0,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$\n\n![](images/ff/ff3c1028da35afad6c215e88863665b5bd6647d2507af2344f9f6710f0ad6373.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84882843-a6bf-570d-81bd-3c32fd39e2ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288922} {:answer/id #uuid "a3336029-0fc7-53ad-b948-fef6a876ad8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289121} {:answer/id #uuid "c49435c4-5090-5fd6-9aaf-96e93ac4b249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289331} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ce2c3e-2091-5106-8ffa-ba30439c81ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289423} {:answer/id #uuid "e20e6b63-f5f2-52c9-8ae3-8c37fe98e719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289331} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf5ce62-8220-592b-b4bb-34fd768d7244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289897}] :question/id #uuid "9c069a6c-f832-5b32-bd66-0a3743eeeafa" :question/math-academy-id "q-225167" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=(u-v)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,(u-v)^{2}) \\\\  & =(0+v,\\,(0-v)^{2}) \\\\  & =(v,\\,{v}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y={v}^{2},\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (0+1,(0-1)^{2})=(1,1) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (0+0,(0-0)^{2})=(0,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](images/d5/d5238b62454c6422988e55f0f4ab9183387725fed037df2a688d351b568ea5c1.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e97e49e-99a8-5167-95a0-4c3a2aa2b214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,-1)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288228} {:answer/id #uuid "27c2c113-8813-50c2-98ea-35f094997ebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288296} {:answer/id #uuid "370f9d77-be30-5124-88d9-5efd241282bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288404} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0618de-1ff4-56fe-a934-7f77d906a294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,-1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288667} {:answer/id #uuid "85690df4-4d70-55f9-838f-5da6f773888e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288933} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb1a6c5-6f69-563f-88b4-ffb836efd328" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289798}] :question/id #uuid "804dcc9f-4962-5beb-bc3d-f0bb08f62de0" :question/math-academy-id "q-225168" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v-2,\\,y={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u+v-2,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =(u+1-2,\\,{u}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(u-1,\\,{u}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y={u}^{2}-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={(x+1)}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(-1,-1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{3}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,1)\\to (1+1-2,\\,{1}^{2}-{1}^{2})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(0,1)\\to (0+1-2,\\,{0}^{2}-{1}^{2})=(-1,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{3}$ is an arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,-1).$\n\n![](images/b1/b139c5b531191bc8aafbb14ad261989ef374503c63f54a567e695e287d4359be.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d42c473-b845-56f6-91ed-2f7569cdadf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from   $(1,1)$ to $(0,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401903} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd13488-86ae-53ef-94b1-8445c40feae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,2)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401904} {:answer/id #uuid "2eaf5793-614c-59c2-8266-69fcc04e0964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401905} {:answer/id #uuid "1cea9769-6304-556d-88e7-17fabaf6e8d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401906} {:answer/id #uuid "132e7131-949c-5374-87b4-fafc2b9fee75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,2)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401905} :answer-field/id #uuid "47132c57-ee0e-5ec3-b95e-67a15913786d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401902}] :question/id #uuid "5bd99021-9f7b-5102-9e9a-342e79f39167" :question/math-academy-id "q-225228" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{v-1}{u+1},\\,y=(u+v-1)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(\\frac{v-1}{u+1},\\,(u+v-1)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{v-1}{0+1},\\,(0+v-1)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,(v-1)^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v-1,\\,y=(v-1)^{2}+1,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=t-1,\\,y=(t-1)^{2}+1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (\\frac{1-1}{0+1},(0+1-1)^{2}+1)=(0,1) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (\\frac{0-1}{0+1},(0+0-1)^{2}+1)=(-1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$\n\n![](images/69/69a2a8ab136360c01d1a17a58dc2fc4864ae2089330a551df913b200d923f087.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3dd863-4c20-526b-baf8-1dd1aaa12685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,-1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407180} {:answer/id #uuid "339db576-bc8c-5b76-9e7a-ca3861bc68aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407183} {:answer/id #uuid "44ffdafc-3200-56d7-8223-b6163e910bc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407184} {:answer/id #uuid "64581119-0bb0-5b9d-9448-7470ca81dcea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,-1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407190} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fa2dfd-1a44-5050-a80a-0ba6f29b907d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407184} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7f7c06-e324-58f6-9f45-6e2ead76ed4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407207}] :question/id #uuid "49191016-a732-5d67-86a5-31f2333ebbd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-225169" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}{v}^{2},\\,y=u-v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}{v}^{2},\\,u-v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2},\\,u-1) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=u-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)=t-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=(y+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,-1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{3}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,1)\\to ({1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2},\\,1-1)=(1,0) \\\\  & T:(0,1)\\to ({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2},\\,0-1)=(0,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{3}$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,-1).$\n\n![](images/d8/d8861cf6be2cb40702ec6f97d75dbb55e50cdde32a5152428dcdeecf839f270a.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157249 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157026 :question/id #uuid "d08f8e35-c4e7-5eef-aca5-175585760433" :question/math-academy-id "e-15065" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = uv, \\qquad y = u(u+v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta.$ So, to map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_1,$ where we have $v = 0$ and $0 \\leq u \\leq 1.$ Substituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(u(0),\\,u(u+0)) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_2.$ Here, we have $u = 1$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 1.$ Substituting $u = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(1(v),\\,1(1+v)) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1+v), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_3.$ Here, we have $v = 1$ and $1 \\geq u \\geq 0.$ Substituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(u(1),\\,u(u+1)) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}+u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_4.$ Here, we have $u = 0$ and $1 \\geq v \\geq 0.$ Substituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(0v,\\,0(0+v)) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $\\Delta{:}$\n\n![](images/79/793412e250ac4f26966293771306f28bb037685dd7886dc386706f23dcaee8af.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0659196-7133-54fe-8b61-0453676189b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/4367227477398d54e05d978d1499aea7f0dabb42ab9223b21cbec43c5ada55c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153899} {:answer/id #uuid "c3533e64-d456-5711-afb3-7f42cdb317e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/12ded9e8002035e126364e41d0dffc1abd6332b3496b4db9da4a68e116e7492e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153900} {:answer/id #uuid "0da0b259-3fa0-543f-a443-ea24739aef3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/25f5503b720084033be843eb1bce7adfe3bc59c39f5f634074d4e414079c9c36.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153901} {:answer/id #uuid "30653f6a-0b46-5f44-8a79-b093ef6a9253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153902} {:answer/id #uuid "558206b9-a9c5-57df-b604-173cf6f2ea85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/33293e18f3b42154b3a21dd627e7f06fbcf443b1251ac1a9ce6e5cb6a47c5971.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153903}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d7004c7f-dc8d-5227-b366-6acfa28dce70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "600a43db-7ad7-59a7-8a4e-811756e9e090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/4367227477398d54e05d978d1499aea7f0dabb42ab9223b21cbec43c5ada55c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389024} {:answer/id #uuid "af57ed6d-87af-5a9e-aba0-d344b0beded2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/12ded9e8002035e126364e41d0dffc1abd6332b3496b4db9da4a68e116e7492e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389025} {:answer/id #uuid "f1817307-acca-5145-b9f8-f957482ffe59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389026} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e1dfd6-e586-58b6-a3b2-fb619c6f4782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/25f5503b720084033be843eb1bce7adfe3bc59c39f5f634074d4e414079c9c36.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389027} {:answer/id #uuid "15d16791-9d02-5cda-8ca2-b435a7d594dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/33293e18f3b42154b3a21dd627e7f06fbcf443b1251ac1a9ce6e5cb6a47c5971.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389026} :answer-field/id #uuid "318f82be-54f2-5f7d-8b28-47015fa276c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389023}] :question/id #uuid "91008bb7-2af5-5c56-89d5-09e110f3df57" :question/math-academy-id "q-160353" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=u(1+{v}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u(1+{0}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1+{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,u(1+{1}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,2u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/46/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4589a8a7-f051-572e-a25b-cba772b96ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/41e864d2554c7007d3e23e7121ac6552cf6a33563b107f71e7d12d43ac7e58bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407185} {:answer/id #uuid "4df51f26-e472-5a52-9d84-60f233e16ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1fbc53395062b956578f583c12f02ec45544eaba9365986e83343cf4ff648167.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407186} {:answer/id #uuid "585890c6-349d-5e4b-9e94-a9bab08010cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8ce9ccb7a714291aaeb31440d0476569355938f79e3aea0bf30c298e5fa8e311.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407189} {:answer/id #uuid "d56a8ed2-f8fb-51b4-ad31-de2d59c40721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bf57b257c2e47d8927e6a441740b503ff4e01931989ee2820f6cfcbec1f8d53.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407197} {:answer/id #uuid "d65a3cae-36d7-5c36-ad61-74f27433b1cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af1a848098711c212031b4559bd496773464a93d192c367b0f7a1c847a1ee15b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/41e864d2554c7007d3e23e7121ac6552cf6a33563b107f71e7d12d43ac7e58bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407185} :answer-field/id #uuid "3008a3ae-1a0a-5c5c-aeb0-57e78911dec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407206}] :question/id #uuid "7ae05696-5f01-5ceb-ade9-b9460da7e939" :question/math-academy-id "q-160570" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=uv,\\,y={u}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u\\cdot 0,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1\\cdot v,\\,{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u\\cdot 1,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0\\cdot v,\\,{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n \n\n![](images/68/6812a33686ab2647cd0f5d586152c68770ebfcb1b8d85cedd98f6839a8364afd.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005be913-c4c4-5c8a-94ae-a52d1df508fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cdd115f6defaabc72aaaeb8a6a1642e088860f281a847acacdb635f71a26be08.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275482} {:answer/id #uuid "29e3458d-9217-5083-bdf9-74d60c2e5834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e7c699d1d96e60f031a6fb006e4ae5ae1abd9898f574ab075d4e4a15a4958634.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275855} {:answer/id #uuid "41870c0a-78e6-52d4-90bc-909907ddedb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/61/6114f6e6fec1d7d0a4d41cfba15f3fa5b2a28ef5c288ad4848d6f0fab1ca01a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276053} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5ac2e8-8957-5598-a847-010a01016082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/225339084f34f53e3a406bb8804d31c0fb5f288c6d4456125926a0d0b1116122.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276742} {:answer/id #uuid "f3172ef1-1e96-5f76-8e6e-084f29b48523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fc/fc78afa81f267849dd5546919074c8bfcab6b35430358abaa6e2c9567c599ee0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/61/6114f6e6fec1d7d0a4d41cfba15f3fa5b2a28ef5c288ad4848d6f0fab1ca01a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276053} :answer-field/id #uuid "03ca7752-6755-55b6-b23d-b5d7d2f7ef6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277732}] :question/id #uuid "abca845b-bdf8-5f44-9d9f-d46002292324" :question/math-academy-id "q-160629" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y={v}^{2}-{u}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+0,\\,{0}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,-{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v+1,\\,{v}^{2}-1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,{1}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,1-{u}^{2}), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/61/6114f6e6fec1d7d0a4d41cfba15f3fa5b2a28ef5c288ad4848d6f0fab1ca01a9.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14025dbd-ceee-56b6-8593-32a680555fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5c/5ced1d3826236965d91ac2de999398e4f7390f0fbf84619ae87c1d2a96370625.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275663} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d97e0a-3e72-54ba-bdcc-00c8ba6ce005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/aa/aa0d4fc0d276619b071a32fe727e7647ff29700bb34ec840a93cacc9088db1af.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276981} {:answer/id #uuid "d32ae52c-c363-5744-9d02-cd6b6b9a1dd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/2523e3cb51fea9c58b2076d69c89d21503341c6cbd4ca0db56d6b92455a40340.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277339} {:answer/id #uuid "ef182560-c44c-5837-85ff-1aa146bb67fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/be/be160b4308b9bc676e1fb8033a1200e5af818007cc1f48661339b1c8d29a58db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277578} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d8d948-3194-5c91-bf2b-cd6058a9b565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a687a8434e9643eabcdf3e1600a28eb805f35fd7821936effcb28677366e91ef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5c/5ced1d3826236965d91ac2de999398e4f7390f0fbf84619ae87c1d2a96370625.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275663} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbde598e-cfec-56aa-91a4-bd5b88ec6163" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278083}] :question/id #uuid "b37ce52d-e566-5093-8b6a-19371d4f2b74" :question/math-academy-id "q-160635" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=uv,\\,y=-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u(0),\\,-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u(1),\\,-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,-1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/5c/5ced1d3826236965d91ac2de999398e4f7390f0fbf84619ae87c1d2a96370625.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278328 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121b5fbd-4b53-5c05-942a-885790475908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/589601eccdc398c685c3d595689be60a3482df49e592292a620e9380b0664419.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275642} {:answer/id #uuid "275ade6b-7b77-53dd-813e-0b28f564489a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c91246add81f732ebca0ed0de3a32de9c6310bd889b890746374232bc2c25700.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275837} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb35728-b63d-52eb-8060-c1dbbb5ce5b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/25098bb3cdab08abbfcc68e2a703e38df2abc07e562f6bfddee9e219135933e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275875} {:answer/id #uuid "9dda899a-27da-51c1-9b7e-317ae8da3b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1e/1eeaf3fc507fa7cb5ef5e28e05abf76f113e8253872446bc69c1640022ec780e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276895} {:answer/id #uuid "e340f382-6e79-51be-b7b3-8ab79da14e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e9/e9bbbef3a9ba9bbb3e305201281c82a46fe139972451686c8571cfc4d8b9fd26.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c91246add81f732ebca0ed0de3a32de9c6310bd889b890746374232bc2c25700.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275837} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e3337aa-e22d-5061-9921-73a1f1c26f9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277851}] :question/id #uuid "66e91d5c-c11c-5279-b02d-a2d28e6799de" :question/math-academy-id "q-160641" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u(v-u),\\,y=-u.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u(0-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(-{u}^{2},\\,-u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a left-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(-1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(1(v-1),\\,-1) &  \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,-1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(-1,-1)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u(1-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u-{u}^{2},\\,-u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a left-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(0(v-0),\\,-0) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0).$\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/c9/c91246add81f732ebca0ed0de3a32de9c6310bd889b890746374232bc2c25700.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bd9f9d6-0406-5b60-bf3c-c7fb79ebaf40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/72e271d6a2282fdf7cdf852308f569de848b8bdc71935e1487969f9ea7ac3eb0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373904} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c07d75-8dc0-5472-b4ee-118ce7ee6da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e3/e3cfcf4c6cd7bcc8298a7e3d8c76f4add3f61689cecc1dacb9fa8a48db5a042b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373905} {:answer/id #uuid "4157d539-f77d-5f54-8554-c73457ce6de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/64/648fa48df1bd648d9fbf48efc966698228416ad9bf3a0f69045031e955601d61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373906} {:answer/id #uuid "c5415f6b-51be-511e-8dcf-7bc985ea09e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3b/3ba341827d95d261f8def4462a11282051b3605795459343441f9f35284faf33.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373907} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8fcb79-14f8-54fd-8a6b-4be84dab14e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0f/0fd03370c63540a8dc9336305bbf917239f02afce2f73371715d6489eee31c9b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/64/648fa48df1bd648d9fbf48efc966698228416ad9bf3a0f69045031e955601d61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373906} :answer-field/id #uuid "515b035c-875d-5a93-a2bf-bdd77d9642fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373903}] :question/id #uuid "e4de9b2d-c42a-5fe3-94b3-1b2caac795c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-160636" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u{v}^{2},\\,y={u}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u({0}^{2}),\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1{v}^{2},\\,{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =({v}^{2},\\,1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u({1}^{2}),\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0{v}^{2},\\,{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/64/648fa48df1bd648d9fbf48efc966698228416ad9bf3a0f69045031e955601d61.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca8e913-bddb-5b6e-84f9-856530467337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4e/4ee5f1df8f74ca6412d4f02fba7258b2b672a8bc80ef13617d4b327f8859e007.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389058} {:answer/id #uuid "6e15c43c-6c08-5aab-a751-b59a2c11ace9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/941f34cbed3f3d6a9957b8520b36d6cbdc20c6241d2e407381ac86d3241a3349.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389059} {:answer/id #uuid "248188a9-928e-5c87-ad62-46c21aa9491c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/66/662c1937641b84653920b9c4e8cfac8168470d077641d001ebd258a0ffff34ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389060} {:answer/id #uuid "e01d5167-cb9b-5f33-8166-a5cf7601da02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6d/6d514d79a788e4512370310eab371dc334e37fc20aaffdf3ad93ebe632b84d4b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389061} {:answer/id #uuid "83f448db-dc49-5111-9d66-3f3b3fa4d186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7f/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7f/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389062} :answer-field/id #uuid "c10a4451-d9b8-5b5f-a00f-c4f4fbd71af3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389057}] :question/id #uuid "6b264343-8b82-57fd-9f36-893b59fb2172" :question/math-academy-id "q-160628" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=-u,\\,y={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(-1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-1,\\,{1}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-1,\\,1-{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(-1,1)$ to the point $(-1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-0,\\,{0}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/7f/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaf80778-6e4d-52c2-a5db-4a2ef680da77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/19/19650cf966c3c455fa859877b4718c08aea8a343876e5817c625618098a84730.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389065} {:answer/id #uuid "c91635d8-70ba-5406-b235-c716cd8cb5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9dc7559632cbecf450389f362ca478d40576ac9646489fda281519c48f49eb16.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389066} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d4992f-3bd8-5933-9744-bd2784689f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/565227489ba6ae7a657a4b2f1e0133bc117e0e666557f58f59ec0272ad8c574e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389067} {:answer/id #uuid "d9925307-ecfb-5b78-8679-f58cb68b9e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389068} {:answer/id #uuid "f94a3624-0a44-5d1c-a294-b6849858edb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eb6908aea50ad1c314efefdaaf922a8c637673a38f76ee8aadcbdc361f44ff78.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389068} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4309d7-1981-54e2-82f3-7e4f837d1ae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389064}] :question/id #uuid "5e3c0bea-5d6e-5a25-bcb3-abdcd207f44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-160632" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y=u.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{0}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,1) &  \\\\  & =(1+{v}^{2},\\,1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(2,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{1}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+1,\\,u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(2,1)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,u) &  \\\\  & =({0}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,0) &  \\\\  & =({v}^{2},\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/56/56f11a4e05de6f090f94d38550b4cd21f042c1acc5d9ae67242de5fe3e9c2e50.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c41f979-3dd4-5a07-b9d1-7abcad42f253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/48a8dc30e5e9dcbd14673f1b120af39d706ca9cfe60dcdfa4a45b5bf2d33f0fc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389072} {:answer/id #uuid "143cba19-e67e-53ad-9f8e-752d3c856632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c50d53ea64f295bed17ba1f9143374cec7dd7b9ff5128861c17ab3730d5d3f79.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389073} {:answer/id #uuid "0a208d98-ea42-5195-8140-855e635b8261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4a2614c282a7cd82ff52ecacd7135f9c95bce5df5cfa9a06f2824b63c60c6eb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389074} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f9b1c9-0f68-5311-9f52-7592e7ceb3d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ab/abdf69cc018c8a0bc7ce6e73f3cca70cb26400ccc5f618f94e05620fc2767bb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389075} {:answer/id #uuid "a18dd9cb-2ae4-5ea9-b8bd-4c6d1b80e29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/0204d60ae19340ddd417982aef69b1fa67c6f6c3ac78142735726aeea7fb2b24.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4a2614c282a7cd82ff52ecacd7135f9c95bce5df5cfa9a06f2824b63c60c6eb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389074} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d2a36fa-1df6-5380-bca3-547e89ee67e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389071}] :question/id #uuid "17778faf-867d-5bf4-a58a-bee309a2767d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160634" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v(u+1),\\,y={v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v(u+1),\\,{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0(u+1),\\,{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v(u+1),\\,{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v(1+1),\\,{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(2v,\\,{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(2,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v(u+1),\\,{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1(u+1),\\,{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(2,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v(u+1),\\,{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v(0+1),\\,{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/4a/4a2614c282a7cd82ff52ecacd7135f9c95bce5df5cfa9a06f2824b63c60c6eb2.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59020315-291c-59c3-9224-961256ce3b94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cf/cfcee7d6c19b748385a35291ab582e7ce0dace9a1207d014b64165caa6d3bc2a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416659} {:answer/id #uuid "5cdefaa7-89d2-5114-a2e1-0fce719d5db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/217244e03a7f3eb3d47d936a337de962d680d477ed944a75f7e74d426b53ff8a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416660} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5ebdc7-9d12-52cf-8d15-a55e4c96ac2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a60a2532e8cfea8044ddc5e52ef65a8fc83808fc8d35217130d243fe77e8c350.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416661} {:answer/id #uuid "9dcdde3a-a348-5fc5-968e-c1ee5b390866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2c/2c1d6e726da49ac8c398357ee00cbb53706a13a6baec25d35a3c594192d804eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416664} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5779ec-3893-54d8-8fd5-cb71c18cf754" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/166fff724178c7285d6256c5b6f68c4181b66c246426e75759e196b2f2b3c0a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cf/cfcee7d6c19b748385a35291ab582e7ce0dace9a1207d014b64165caa6d3bc2a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416659} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b03f791-a25e-5d56-8bc4-3d32145b2ec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416667}] :question/id #uuid "b9cdc646-22ad-53d7-894a-78ecf8a59507" :question/math-academy-id "q-160633" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=uv.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,uv) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u(0)) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,0), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,uv) &  \\\\  & =({1}^{2},\\,1v) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,v), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,uv) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u(1)) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a right-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,uv) &  \\\\  & =({0}^{2},\\,0v) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/cf/cfcee7d6c19b748385a35291ab582e7ce0dace9a1207d014b64165caa6d3bc2a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2082dc17-8677-5072-8728-eb1279bccdcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/eec7dbae504f01dda802a07eb200fcf9a1823ffd3a71665b87936b24e7e5525a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436378} {:answer/id #uuid "69783711-c08d-571c-86a6-8b131065e28d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/86/868f4a5fc5f2e2d30dc5d1a693cb8df7bd6cbdad90483d806a3c08afc521d1ff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436381} {:answer/id #uuid "816f6705-f146-5bfb-a96a-c00de5846b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/7215170dcbc618f7a006cc4aedd65bdc36dd8bccd708bbe9784dcbfc4974efea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436384} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba99d86-24e8-5ca6-94cd-b5ea2ff49d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3b/3b06791c21bcb41f654c1d01f8e4b9cb58df36cf76615ce0b41a0245835c38d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436385} {:answer/id #uuid "afc4090a-a50d-5cfd-b9cf-41e1abf26da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cc/ccd7576940650b3c1ba7a0b0c404b2dc39d287261d77f556f63b8166496c69df.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cc/ccd7576940650b3c1ba7a0b0c404b2dc39d287261d77f556f63b8166496c69df.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436387} :answer-field/id #uuid "d96343de-f671-5ac9-bcd5-9b8c7a8dc39b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436388}] :question/id #uuid "015f5e64-8ccf-5a6a-8fbf-8ce2c8d20eda" :question/math-academy-id "q-160631" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+0,\\,-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,0), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1+v,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(2,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,-1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(2,-1)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0+v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/cc/ccd7576940650b3c1ba7a0b0c404b2dc39d287261d77f556f63b8166496c69df.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2832 :topic/title "Nonlinear Transformations of Plane Regions"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204941} :activity/id #uuid "d6b8ef6d-fb62-5d97-89ca-bf7d505d9828" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205161 :tutorial/content "In a directed multigraph $G=(V,E)$ with $n$ vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$, the **adjacency matrix** $A$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$ matrix (table), where the entry $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the number of edges from $v_i$ to $v_j$ (in this order).\n\nNotice that, according to this definition:\n\n- If there are no edges connecting $v_i$ and $v_j$, the corresponding entry is $0$.\n- For $i=j$, we count the number of loops at the vertex $v_i$.\n- The adjacency matrix of a directed graph is *not* symmetric in general.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to build the adjacency matrix for the graph below.\n\n![](images/ef/ef213f4c4dda9a2622acc399327941a3e531188debe8d6c03d40e3f456cd6fee.png)\n\nLet's examine the edges of the graph to determine the matrix entries step by step.\n\n- There is an edge from $v_1$ to $v_2$. Hence, we write $1$ in the entry $a_{12}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ |  | $1$ |  |  |\n| $v_2$ |  |  |  |  |\n| $v_3$ |  |  |  |  |\n| $v_4$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- There are two edges from $v_2$ to $v_3$. Hence, we write $2$ in the entry $a_{23}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ |  | $1$ |  |  |\n| $v_2$ |  |  | $2$ |  |\n| $v_3$ |  |  |  |  |\n| $v_4$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- There is an edge from $v_4$ to $v_4$ (i.e., the loop). Hence, we write $1$ in the entry $a_{44}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ |  | $1$ |  |  |\n| $v_2$ |  |  | $2$ |  |\n| $v_3$ |  |  |  |  |\n| $v_4$ |  |  |  | $1$ |\n\n- Finally, we write $0$ for all remaining entries where no edges exist.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $v_2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n| $v_3$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $v_4$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nThe adjacency matrix is completed. In the usual matrix notation, we can write this as follows.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c1340fd4-0d5c-56a3-bb41-a6acfcb45dd2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11019 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "98e5cd28-6e84-55fc-aad1-2a5db281dfa0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204942}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204046 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa6b9b63-30e8-5ede-81ad-1efebdc80bb5"} :step/id #uuid "cc321515-08ff-50f3-8200-bae628aa5e7c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204943}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204943 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205162 :tutorial/content "In a directed weighted graph $G=(V,E)$ without parallel edges, with $n$ vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$, the **adjacency matrix** $A$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$ matrix, where the entry $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the *weight* of the edge from $v_i$ to $v_j$ (in this order). If no edge exists between $v_i$ and $v_j$, then the entry $a_{ij}$ is considered to be $\\infty$.\n\nNotice that, according to this definition:\n\n- The entry $a_{ii}$ is the weight of the loop at the vertex $v_i$, or $\\infty$ if no loop exists.\n- The adjacency matrix of a directed, weighted graph is *not* symmetric in general.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to build the adjacency matrix for the graph below.\n\n![](images/02/02bdab7a2baa29c6d688dbe0208223300169bedaed03859810b6beb1bb4f1d54.png)\n\nLet's examine the edges of the graph to determine the matrix entries step by step.\n\n- The weight of the edge $v_1 \\to v_2$ is $2$. Hence, we write $2$ in the entry $a_{12}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ |  | $2$ |  |\n| $v_2$ |  |  |  |\n| $v_3$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The weight of the edge $v_1 \\to v_3$ is $6$. Hence, we write $6$ in the entry $a_{13}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ |  | $2$ | $6$ |\n| $v_2$ |  |  |  |\n| $v_3$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The weight of the loop at the vertex $v_1$ is $12$. Hence, we write $12$ in the entry $a_{11}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $12$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n| $v_2$ |  |  |  |\n| $v_3$ |  |  |  |\n\n- Finally, we write $\\infty$ for all remaining entries where no edges exist.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $12$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n| $v_2$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ |\n| $v_3$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nThe adjacency matrix is completed. Using an alternative notation, we can write it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 2 & 6 \\\\ ∞ & ∞ & ∞ \\\\ ∞ & ∞ & ∞\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "90306a3d-b132-522c-a00e-0f337038db19" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11020 :tutorial/title "Adjacency Matrices of Directed Weighted Graphs"} :step/id #uuid "0331a2e7-e123-5e00-8d0b-59df6c3a82a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204944}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204944 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13ec8d43-2684-52f1-ac9e-f32e3cb0a5f4"} :step/id #uuid "af7e0716-de1c-584b-993c-5e04ded35198" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204945}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204945 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204048 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eac3d6c-8d31-58c9-832d-772d81432f12"} :step/id #uuid "ec5195b2-b281-55c2-80a9-f39d4e44a5bf"}] :activity/title "Adjacency Matrices of Directed Graphs" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204168}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049389 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204046 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203794 :question/id #uuid "4d8c2d2a-83f4-56ff-b7c2-199eaf786d01" :question/math-academy-id "e-15996" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe directed graph with vertices $v_1$, $v_2$, $v_3$ (in this exact order) has the adjacency matrix shown above. Draw the graph that corresponds to this adjacency matrix." :question/worked-solution "Given a fixed order of vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$ in a directed graph, the *adjacency matrix* $A$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$-matrix (table), where the element $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column represents the number of edges from $v_i$ and $v_j$ (in this order).\n\nNote that, unlike undirected graphs, the adjacent matrix of a directed graph is *not* symmetric in general.\n\nFirst, we draw our vertices on the plane.\n\n![](images/43/439d3222f5350ec99fb0b67327fdf3b36bde219b7462a3f1c61c204e68bbbd08.png)\n\nNow, let's examine the adjacency matrix $A$.\n\nSince this is a directed graph, the matrix may not be symmetric about the main diagonal. Hence, we need to iterate through all the nonzero entries, row by row.\n\n- Consider the $1$st row. Since $a_{11}=1$, there is a loop at $v_1$. Additionally, since $a_{12}=1$ and $a_{13}=1$, we have the edges $v_1 \\to v_2$ and $v_1 \\to v_3$.\n\n- Consider the $2$nd row. Since $a_{21}=1$ and $a_{23}=1$, we have the edges $v_2 \\to v_1$ and $v_2 \\to v_3$.\n- Consider the $3$rd row. Since $a_{32}=1$, we have the edge $v_3 \\to v_2$. Additionally, since $a_{33}=1$, there is a loop at $v_3$.\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fae1aa9256f2aab7fa91b322c9bae27635bc614c2d4a907914443893e323d66e.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa6b9b63-30e8-5ede-81ad-1efebdc80bb5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "897cc5e1-d6e1-59a3-8b6c-1f5c4ef3e31b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/434bba020a73e80113d805caae06f9e33f9e5b4a7919a1dc53ed8c2773f85481.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202390} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7de943-b8b7-5ff2-ad01-c13987aa953f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3b/3b9ec09944858de99ebb2239b685937d10a01b889c1addca4adda20a3151aa89.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202391} {:answer/id #uuid "06719109-b216-5349-84a8-daed2b02157b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/61/6167287fe54cf3d1f268c08a1bf77e3d7d8406f01586f1906c4cd7c1034187ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202392} {:answer/id #uuid "442bcae7-51e7-52bc-9307-9da5ff2c47f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/5800ef3da284e65f87f87681e5cba1e5219e8c2c57966a180c978599790967a5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202393} {:answer/id #uuid "780bdcfe-0aea-5949-ab5b-5f004bb9792a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/38/38bd8a37ddf7f3e6b72b2219c47b7048de0ebaecbef16dcfb1198dffd4e0cb8a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202394}] :answer-field/id #uuid "136ad9f7-d6eb-56b1-bf7d-61c9bf4539d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202389}] :question/id #uuid "f5867541-b595-51cc-9f9e-d91235fbfc37" :question/math-academy-id "q-243167" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe directed graph with vertices $v_{1}$, $v_{2}$, $v_{3}$ (in this exact order) has the adjacency matrix shown above. Which of the following represents the given graph?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "bf775ef3-9fd1-5497-842d-fa2aafe1b484" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203085} {:answer-field/id #uuid "dacd6d5a-a5d9-5995-91fb-747b0d2fa87a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203086} {:answer-field/id #uuid "47fabdaf-d53b-5982-99f3-fd31aeec260a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203087} {:answer-field/id #uuid "74d85635-2977-51df-a5e1-10e135fd15c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203088}] :question/id #uuid "7d8d0fd5-6d4f-50dc-9b46-4804ffcb870c" :question/math-academy-id "q-258263" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a3c01f71fa7fe31bc24b7eaaf9efda7001f3c1887f951b0e89c80ba98ed5f10c.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix for the graph above:\n\n|  | $v_{1}$ | $v_{2}$ | $v_{3}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_{1}$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $v_{2}$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $v_{3}$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | $0$ |"}] :knowledge-point/title "Building the Adjacency Matrix of a Directed Graph"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204047 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203795 :question/id #uuid "5fbe7d68-227a-5248-b8ce-b922e12d1d6e" :question/math-academy-id "e-15998" :question/problem "![](images/33/335c85b93251993c627056a41695ef364a82c687e14ee38c340b708c52d0cee7.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the above graph:\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ |  | $\\infty$ | $7$ |  |\n| $v_2$ | $5$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ |\n| $v_3$ |  | $3$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ |\n| $v_4$ | $\\infty$ | $11$ | $\\infty$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a fixed order of vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$ in a directed weighted graph without multiple edges, the *adjacency matrix* $A$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$-matrix (table), where the element $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column represents the weight of the arrow from $v_i$ to $v_j$.\n\nNote that, unlike undirected graphs, the adjacent matrix of a directed graph is *not* symmetric in general. Also, if there is no edge from vertex $v_i$ to vertex $v_j$, then we write the symbol $\\infty$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column on the adjacency matrix.\n\nIn our case, the vertices are arranged as $v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4$. Let's examine the edges of our graph.\n\n- There is a loop in the vertex $v_1$ with a weight of $4$. Hence, we write $4$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $1$st column in the adjacency matrix.\n$a_{11}=4$\n\n- There is an edge from $v_1$ to $v_4$ with a weight of $9$. Hence, we write $9$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $4$th column in the adjacency matrix.\n$a_{14}=9$\n- There is no edges from $v_3$ to $v_1$. Hence, we write the symbol $\\infty$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $1$st column in the adjacency matrix.\n$a_{31} = \\infty$\n- There is no loop in the vertex $v_4$. Hence, we write the symbol $\\infty$ in the intersection of the $4$th row and the $4$th column in the adjacency matrix.\n$a_{44}=\\infty$\n\nTherefore, the adjacency matrix is as follows:\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}4}$ | $\\infty$ | $7$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$ |\n| $v_2$ | $5$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ |\n| $v_3$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\infty}$ | $3$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ |\n| $v_4$ | $\\infty$ | $11$ | $\\infty$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\infty}$ |"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13ec8d43-2684-52f1-ac9e-f32e3cb0a5f4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9023684c-86ee-5d0c-afcd-b5eaa230456e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202395} {:answer-field/id #uuid "fe5d8e3f-993b-54bb-b443-a1520ae99239" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202396} {:answer-field/id #uuid "0c567d4f-f06e-550a-b618-6f5d3202aa5c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202397} {:answer-field/id #uuid "0d77ae67-4cdc-5bde-8e3f-572d513a8991" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202398}] :question/id #uuid "554a582e-60bf-5d59-95a8-8ca0255cc4bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-243168" :question/problem "![](images/62/62069b679c5c48ab94b334f9044555f71b6a59dd3a0f3f09114736fb75735328.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the above graph:\n\n|  | $v_{1}$ | $v_{2}$ | $v_{3}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_{1}$ | $∞$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| $v_{2}$ | {{field-3}} | $∞$ | $∞$ |\n| $v_{3}$ | $∞$ | $∞$ | {{field-4}} |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "df1c46e7-b36f-5914-a76e-b37ed399180f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203081} {:answer-field/id #uuid "177a5b29-c9ee-5f09-bc64-f1a9c0010395" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203082} {:answer-field/id #uuid "17975ec6-6c04-5d01-b4f3-a0875808e77e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203083} {:answer-field/id #uuid "5bd03c82-9089-5e78-8cff-98f545770eaf" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203084}] :question/id #uuid "e13ee731-2495-5dfe-9f4c-2f661af74aa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-258096" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d803bfe52a023fceeda7a93d1cd7d6e6d83b02a366a38322e490b039c433155a.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the above graph:\n\n|  | $v_{1}$ | $v_{2}$ | $v_{3}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_{1}$ | $∞$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| $v_{2}$ | $∞$ | $∞$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $v_{3}$ | $∞$ | $∞$ | {{field-4}} |"}] :knowledge-point/title "Building the Adjacency Matrix of a Directed Weighted Graph (Without Multiple Edges)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204048 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203855 :question/id #uuid "dda3ebf8-ea58-5eaf-b586-b1f9a1ed3ff2" :question/math-academy-id "e-17277" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}∞ & ∞ & 1 \\\\ ∞ & ∞ & 2 \\\\ ∞ & ∞ & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe directed weighted graph with vertices $v_1$, $v_2$, $v_3$ (in this exact order) has the adjacency matrix shown above. Draw the graph that corresponds to this adjacency matrix." :question/worked-solution "Given a fixed order of vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$ in a directed weighted graph without multiple edges, the *adjacency matrix* $A$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$-matrix (table), where the element $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column represents the weight of the arrow from $v_i$ to $v_j$. When the symbol $\\infty$ appears at this intersection, there is no edge from vertex $v_i$ to vertex $v_j$.\n\nNote that, unlike undirected graphs, the adjacent matrix of a directed graph is *not* symmetric in general.\n\nFirst, we draw our vertices on the plane.\n\n![](images/ce/cec451b57a44952d9ffc5d7167431d9c8e72cd0be66b8dacd2ef520e5bcbe519.png)\n\nNow, let's examine the adjacency matrix $A$.\n\nSince this is a directed graph, the matrix may not be symmetric about the main diagonal. Hence, we must iterate through all the entries different from the symbol $\\infty$, row by row.\n\n- Consider the $1$st row. Since $a_{13}=1$, we have the edge $v_1 \\to v_3$ with a weight of $1$.\n- Consider the $2$nd row. Since $a_{23}=2$, we have the edge $v_2 \\to v_3$ with a weight of $2$.\n- Consider the $3$rd row. Since $a_{33}=3$, there is a loop at $v_1$ with a weight of $3$.\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e0215bdb27759d6d1f812c254442224cb2229aa80bd10273d133c943f61e6ae.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eac3d6c-8d31-58c9-832d-772d81432f12" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f248f6da-189c-5ec3-9a0b-505e9e7c31d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/76/76461aab0328c84c67e55cf94aa918e210991dddd7b424f198ed4e2a233f3fb1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203090} {:answer/id #uuid "118f87c3-48df-55c4-a09f-4124ceead768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3ff2b9bbc5d34d3ae82480ee11e459dfc61ba8ade4d7b9d33139f4d06dbeebf1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203091} {:answer/id #uuid "55b6467a-8302-5aec-91d7-6bc977bf46c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f2/f2f0ef68dbb58c5dfcf40957335e328cd8bb087d7f15dfe2ba150072c2453338.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203092} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc9477f-9d22-5faa-9f8e-8635dfafc2d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b1/b10663517b5befcffa661170ea4f19bba76a1f17dc3840dda694e6f00d1fcb3c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203093} {:answer/id #uuid "79befb6c-b266-533c-a98e-35a5bc812329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b9e8954dfb93e9a72c212385c909ab7b503e98df93c6a6ca4a4cfa0ab01126f4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203094}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e3f3ebab-785b-577e-8af7-d65926506edc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203089}] :question/id #uuid "44f374d8-3dc1-5e39-92a2-d8f8f9f42ec4" :question/math-academy-id "q-258287" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & ∞ \\\\ ∞ & ∞ & 5 \\\\ ∞ & ∞ & ∞\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe directed weighted graph with vertices $v_{1}$, $v_{2}$, $v_{3}$ (in this exact order) has the adjacency matrix shown above. Which of the following represents the given graph?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e270e660-546b-5144-a777-501b8f658dfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/53/5357d113dc57f22